%N A399666 Triangle read by rows: The Bell transform of the least common multiple of {1, 2, ..., n}. (LCM(n) = A003418(n)).
%C A399666 For the definition of the Bell transform see A264428.
%H A399666 Peter Luschny, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/User:Peter_Luschny/BellTransform">The Bell transform</a>.
%H A399666 Peter Luschny, <a href="https://github.com/PeterLuschny/Gists/blob/main/BellTransformation.ipynb">Examples of the Bell transform, a SageMath notebook</a>.
%F A399666 T(n, k) = Bell_{n, k}(A003418), where Bell_{n, k} is the partial Bell polynomial, evaluated over the least common multiple of [n], LCM(n). (see Mathematica)
%F A399666 T(n, k) = Sum_{j=0..n-k} binomial(n-1, j) * T(n-j-1, k-1) * LCM(j); T(n, n) = 1, T(n, 0) = 0^n.
%e A399666 Triangle T(n, k) begins:
%e A399666   [0] 1;
%e A399666   [1] 0,   1;
%e A399666   [2] 0,   1,    1;
%e A399666   [3] 0,   2,    3,    1;
%e A399666   [4] 0,   6,   11,    6,    1;
%e A399666   [5] 0,  12,   50,   35,   10,    1;
%e A399666   [6] 0,  60,  202,  225,   85,   15,   1;
%e A399666   [7] 0,  60, 1092, 1372,  735,  175,  21,  1;
%e A399666   [8] 0, 420, 4764, 9436, 6097, 1960, 322, 28, 1;
%e A399666 .
%e A399666 Seen as an array A(n, k) = T(n + k, k):
%e A399666   [0] 1,  1,    1,     1,      1,       1,        1, ... A000012
%e A399666   [1] 0,  1,    3,     6,     10,      15,       21, ... A000217
%e A399666   [2] 0,  2,   11,    35,     85,     175,      322, ... A000914
%e A399666   [3] 0,  6,   50,   225,    735,    1960,     4536, ... A001303
%e A399666   [4] 0, 12,  202,  1372,   6097,   20937,    60249, ...
%e A399666   [5] 0, 60, 1092,  9436,  53172,  226485,   791175, ...
